<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Own Hub privacy manifest. Matches privacy policy v3 sections 3 and 4 and
	  the App Store Connect App Privacy answers. A mismatch between the three is
	  one of the most common rejections.

	  PostHog also receives consented product interaction milestones, a pseudonymous
	  app identifier and SDK device metadata; one fixed diagnostic status log.
	  No advertising, user identification, replay or health values are sent.
	  The existing DeviceID/ProductInteraction declarations also cover this SDK;
	  recheck App Store privacy answers against device traffic before release.
	  WHAT THIS BINARY DOES TODAY. `Firebase.initializeApp` runs on every launch
	  (`lib/app_state/firebase_boot.dart`), and pubspec carries nine Firebase
	  packages: firebase_core, firebase_auth, cloud_firestore, firebase_storage,
	  cloud_functions, firebase_app_check, firebase_crashlytics,
	  firebase_messaging (the one push she can ask for on C14, "your check is
	  done") and firebase_analytics (usage statistics, only if she turns on
	  "Help improve Own Hub"), plus Google ML Kit face detection on the phone. Firebase mints an installation
	  identifier, App Check attests the device, and the women-only check keeps a
	  hashed install id for fraud signals: DeviceID, linked.

	  LINKED to her account, none tracking, purpose AppFunctionality:
	    - PhoneNumber: sign-in by SMS code, and contact by a woman from Own Hub when she asks for a review.
	    - UserID: the Firebase uid her account and documents are keyed by.
	    - DeviceID: Firebase installation id, App Check, hashed install id,
	      and the push token, only if she asks to be told when her check is
	      done (optional, deleted with her account). Purpose also Analytics:
	      the Google Analytics app instance id, only with her consent to
	      usage statistics, reset when she withdraws it. No IDFA (the
	      analytics SDK is FirebaseAnalytics/Core, without AdSupport) and no
	      IDFV (GOOGLE_ANALYTICS_IDFV_COLLECTION_ENABLED is false).
	    - Fitness: workouts, meals, plans, synced once she is verified.
	    - Health: cycle, check-ins, measurements, weight, allergies, only if
	      she turns on "Back up my health data" (off by default, own consent).
	    - SensitiveInfo: pregnancy or postpartum stage (with backup on), and
	      the biometric face match during the check.
	    - Name: only for a woman who applies to coach: her full name, kept
	      with the application for the review (never public). The name read
	      from the ID card is still processed in memory and not kept.
	    - EmailsorTextMessages: chat messages between a member and her
	      coach, kept on our servers (Frankfurt) while both accounts exist,
	      readable by the two of them; staff read a thread only after a report.
	    - PhotosorVideos: both sides of her national ID card and the selfie
	      frames, processed in memory and never stored. Declared anyway. Also,
	      for a woman who applies to coach only: the pictures she picks for
	      the application (profile photo, documents, feedback screenshots,
	      client photos, photos of herself or her work), which ARE stored.
	      The woman reviewer checks each one; on approval a re-encoded copy
	      with no location or other metadata is kept, the original is
	      deleted, and verified members see the approved items.
	    - OtherDataTypes: the verification result, the keyed code of her ID
	      number, and her consent records. Also the coach application text,
	      the public coach portfolio, and the intake form a member sends a
	      coach (health answers in it fall under Health, already declared).
	  NOT LINKED, not tracking, purpose AppFunctionality:
	    - CrashData: Firebase Crashlytics, release builds, and only once she
	      has allowed crash reports: fatal crashes, and non-fatal reports of a
	      server call that failed with a bug's status code. No user identifier
	      is ever set; the only custom keys are the screen id, the app version
	      and the build channel, and the only log lines are fixed words (a
	      source guard test enforces all of it).
	    - PerformanceData: ML Kit performance events.
	  NOT LINKED, not tracking, purpose Analytics, only once she has turned on
	  "Help improve Own Hub" (off by default, its own consent, separate from
	  crash reports; collection is off natively until then):
	    - ProductInteraction: which screens (by id) and steps she uses, from
	      closed vocabularies. Never health, body, cycle, life stage, name,
	      number, ID or anything she typed. No user id is set, so Google
	      Analytics holds it against a random app instance id only.
	    - CoarseLocation: Google Analytics derives an approximate location
	      (country, and city unless granular location is off in the GA
	      property) from the masked IP address. The app reads no location.
	  Training reminders (flutter_local_notifications) are scheduled on the
	  phone and collect nothing: the days and time she picks stay in the
	  app's local snapshot, and no token or schedule leaves the device.
	  There is no combined "health and fitness" key in Apple's list; Fitness
	  and Health are declared separately.

	  NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes for the APP TARGET holds three entries. UserDefaults
	  1C8F.1, for the App Group group.com.ownhub.fitness that home_widget writes the
	  widget payload to (lib/app_state/home_widget_sync.dart). home_widget
	  0.10.0 ships no privacy manifest of its own, so the reason is declared
	  here. The widget extension declares the same reason in its own manifest.
	  FileTimestamp C617.1, for the exercise library's clips and posters kept in
	  the app's own cache folder (lib/features/train/data/exercise_media_store.dart):
	  Dart's file calls read the size and timestamps of those files, inside the
	  app container only. path_provider_foundation ships no manifest of its own.
	  SystemBootTime 35F9.1, for the accelerometer that tilts the welcome and
	  finished art a few pixels (lib/ui/hand/tilt_parallax.dart):
	  sensors_plus 7.1.1 reads ProcessInfo.systemUptime to stamp each reading
	  and its own manifest declares no API types. The accelerometer is CoreMotion,
	  so Info.plist carries NSMotionUsageDescription (localised in the
	  InfoPlist.strings files), and the readings never leave the screen.
	  Every other required-reason API this product touches is touched by a
	  package that ships its own manifest, which Xcode merges into the privacy
	  report at build time: shared_preferences declares NSUserDefaults 1C8F.1,
	  and the Google and MLKit pods declare disk space, file timestamp, boot
	  time and user defaults. If the app target ever reads one of those APIs
	  directly, it belongs here.

	  Verify with Xcode: Product > Archive > Organizer > Generate Privacy
	  Report, and compare it line by line with the store answers.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
